  {
   "_id": 11418,
   "ANSWER": "<p data-end=\"2008\" data-start=\"1821\">In Python, libraries like <code data-end=\"1875\" data-start=\"1861\">automata-lib</code> allow us to define a DFA and simulate inputs.<br data-end=\"1924\" data-start=\"1921\" />\nFor example, define a DFA to accept binary strings divisible by 2 (ending with &lsquo;0&rsquo;).</p>\n\n<pre data-end=\"2354\" data-start=\"2010\">\n\n&nbsp;</pre>\n\n<p>python</p>\n\n<p>from automata.fa.dfa import DFA</p>\n\n<p>dfa = DFA(<br />\n&nbsp; &nbsp; states={&#39;q0&#39;, &#39;q1&#39;},<br />\n&nbsp; &nbsp; input_symbols={&#39;0&#39;, &#39;1&#39;},<br />\n&nbsp; &nbsp; transitions={<br />\n&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &#39;q0&#39;: {&#39;0&#39;: &#39;q0&#39;, &#39;1&#39;: &#39;q1&#39;},<br />\n&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &#39;q1&#39;: {&#39;0&#39;: &#39;q0&#39;, &#39;1&#39;: &#39;q1&#39;}<br />\n&nbsp; &nbsp; },<br />\n&nbsp; &nbsp; initial_state=&#39;q0&#39;,<br />\n&nbsp; &nbsp; final_states={&#39;q0&#39;}<br />\n)</p>\n\n<p>print(dfa.accepts_input(&#39;100&#39;)) &nbsp;# True<br />\nprint(dfa.accepts_input(&#39;101&#39;)) &nbsp;# False<br />\n&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p data-end=\"2452\" data-start=\"2356\">This simulation bridges theory and practice, helping validate automaton logic with real strings.</p>\n",
